> Compare selected digit products with an explicit baseline formula

The native counters below count only actual schoolbook digit-pair
multiplications. The n² column is the exact count that an ordinary n-by-n digit
schoolbook loop would perform; it is a formula baseline, not a measured
alternative implementation. All-one and all-nine operands have the same lengths,
but sum-child carries can change the recursive call tree. Additions,
subtractions, carries, shifts and copies are excluded, so a lower count does not
establish elapsed speed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/karatsuba_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/karatsuba.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <limits>
#include <cmath>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, ones, nines, schoolbook;
    std::print("| n | all-one calls | all-one digit products | all-nine calls | all-nine digit products | " "schoolbook formula |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t n : {16, 32, 33, 64, 65, 128, 256, 512}) {
        const auto a =
            dm::algorithms::karatsuba_trace(std::string(n, '1'),
                                            std::string(n, '1'));
        const auto b =
            dm::algorithms::karatsuba_trace(std::string(n, '9'),
                                            std::string(n, '9'));
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n,
                   a.work.recursive_calls, a.work.digit_products,
                   b.work.recursive_calls, b.work.digit_products, n * n);
        x.push_back(std::log2(n));
        ones.push_back(std::log2(a.work.digit_products));
        nines.push_back(std::log2(b.work.digit_products));
        schoolbook.push_back(std::log2(n * n));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, ones, "ones", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, ones, "", {37, 99, 235},
                                                     4).line(x, nines, "nines",
                                                             {5, 150, 105}, 2).points(x, nines, "", {5, 150, 105}, 4).line(x, schoolbook, "n squared", {220, 38, 38}, 2).title("Actual leaf digit work, not elapsed speed").x_label("log2(digits per operand)").y_label("log2(digit products)").y_limits(7, 20).save_svg("build/karatsuba_work.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | all-one calls | all-one digit products | all-nine calls | all-nine digit products | schoolbook formula |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 16 | 1 | 256 | 1 | 256 | 256 |
| 32 | 1 | 1024 | 1 | 1024 | 1024 |
| 33 | 4 | 834 | 4 | 869 | 1089 |
| 64 | 4 | 3072 | 7 | 2882 | 4096 |
| 65 | 10 | 2692 | 10 | 2795 | 4225 |
| 128 | 13 | 9216 | 25 | 8456 | 16384 |
| 256 | 40 | 27648 | 79 | 25178 | 65536 |
| 512 | 124 | 82754 | 241 | 75344 | 262144 |
\image(figures/karatsuba_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Measured native leaf work is separated from the explicit schoolbook formula.)
\alttext(Two actual digit-product curves can differ through carry-sensitive recursion, while the red formula curve is the n-squared baseline.)

Complete syntax and original input-byte admission precede normalized copies.
Trace defaults cap total original digits and actual recursive entries at one
million each, with maximum admitted depth1024; zero disables the corresponding
optional ceiling. Root depth is zero. Recursion limits are checked on entry
after any argument temporaries have been formed, before that invocation's body.
Thus they are not a peak-memory guarantee. Capacity, temporary vectors, byte
overhead and RAM are excluded. Counter/shape arithmetic is checked, and failed
calls expose no partial trace.

The scalar API owns only its result and per-call digit temporaries. Conservative
live digit storage can grow with operand width times recursion depth for unequal
sizes; trace adds one owned metadata record per actual invocation and complete
original operands. No external dataset is appropriate for this exact decimal
arithmetic example.

Independent Python integers verified 100 decimal products through
298-by-199-digit inputs; 1,921 actual recursion entries passed
parent/role/depth/crossover and leaf-work accounting checks. The same 100
products matched the frozen original kernel. Native tests include all two-digit
integer pairs, closed-form all-nine squares, powers of ten, complete ceilings
and ownership. The shared ten-report build passed 131 native tests, 38 directly
instrumented address/undefined-behaviour sanitizer tests, 89 compiled public
examples, documentation coverage for all 388 scoped definitions and ten
representative clangd hovers. All four complete C++ report programs executed
successfully and their native figures were visually reviewed. Batch receipts are
retained under `build/mepml-conversion/batch-174-183/`.

> Reference

A. Karatsuba and Yu. Ofman (1962),
[Multiplication of many-digital numbers by automatic computers|https://www.mathnet.ru/php/archive.phtml?jrnid=dan&option_lang=eng&paperid=26729&wshow=paper],
Doklady Akademii Nauk SSSR145(2),293–294; English translation in Soviet Physics
Doklady7(1963),595–596. The present decimal hybrid retains its
implementation-specific crossover32 and actual carry behavior.
